  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; the full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "It extends evidence about scaling and downstream performance to a Khmer-specific sequence-to-sequence model and Khmer generation tasks, but does not reproduce held-out-loss scaling measurements.",
    "gate_note": "The paper varies PrahokBART model size and pre-training data amount and reports downstream task scores. These outcomes bear on V6, but are not measurements of held-out pre-training loss or compute-optimal allocations. Its discussion that the big model is approximately optimal for the current token count, and its expectation that more data would require a larger model, cite or invoke prior scaling-law work rather than test those claims. Corpus perplexity in Table 5 is for a 5-gram character-level frequency model evaluating corpus cleaning, not a language-model scaling sweep.",
    "test_reason": "The study varies PrahokBART size (62M vs 211M) and pre-training data (39M, 401M, 4.2B tokens) and reports downstream Khmer/English generation scores. This bears on V6, but only weakly: there are two size points and three data points, no fitted curve, no prediction, and no link to pre-training loss. The model-size optimality claim cites Hoffmann et al. and is not tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors report that the larger model and more pre-training data improve downstream scores. They assert, citing Hoffmann et al. rather than testing it, that 211M parameters is about optimal for 4.2B tokens. They also say doubling data to 8B tokens would need about 400M parameters. Their main conclusions concern linguistic modules (normalization, word segmentation) and beating mBART50.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "This is a Khmer-specific sequence-to-sequence pre-training and fine-tuning study reporting downstream task scores, not a held-out-loss scaling experiment or a fixed-compute allocation sweep.",
    "method_sensitivity": "",
    "fits_no_version": [],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "n",
    "uncertainty_reported": "y",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/hour/prahokbart",
    "peer_reviewed": "International Conference on Computational Linguistics",
    "problems": "The scaling evidence consists of a limited number of model sizes and pre-training data amounts, and reports downstream metrics rather than held-out pre-training loss. It does not fit a scaling curve or test predictions on larger runs.",
    "rows_left_out": "No relevant results left out. Other reported measures, including corpus-cleaning perplexity, tokenizer metrics, and the normalization and word-segmentation ablations, do not measure the scaling or downstream scale relationship covered by the tested version.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No held-out pre-training loss measured across parameters, tokens or compute."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "No fits on small runs and no extrapolation to larger runs."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No compute-optimal sweep; the near-optimality of 211M parameters for 4.2B tokens is asserted from Hoffmann et al., not measured."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No fixed-parameter comparison of shapes. Base and big differ in width and heads at different sizes."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "Fine-tuning transfer gains are reported only at two or three scale points, with no power-law form tested."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "No fitted predictive relation, no extrapolation, and no relation of downstream scores to pre-training loss. Only monotonic improvement over two or three points is shown."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "No fitting choices or exponents compared."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "The evidence for V6 is thin: two model sizes and three data sizes, with no curve fit. The mBART50 comparison (611M parameters, lower scores) is not a scaling test, since its data, vocabulary and depth differ; it is correctly not recorded as a row. The Table 5 perplexities come from a 5-gram KenLM model used to evaluate corpus cleaning, not from language-model scaling. Rows r15–r18 duplicate r2, r4, r5 and r6."
  },
